\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The algebra of finite invariant functions}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{For a finite group and a \vocabk{scalar frame}{fdip-010X} at
each site, the algebra of gauge-invariant #{k}-valued functions of
the joint configuration is canonically isomorphic to
##{k^{\,G^E/(G^\circ)^X}.}
Here the reference scalar is fixed, #{k} is any commutative unital
ring, and the quotient consists of residual orbits of whole link
tuples. The indicators #{\mathbf 1_C} of these orbits form an
indexed #{k}-basis. Their pointwise product is zero for distinct
orbits, and each is idempotent.}

\proof{
\p{Restrict an invariant function to the constant reference
scalar. By \ref{fdip-0112} this assigns one value to each
residual orbit, and each assignment extends uniquely to an
invariant function by giving the same value to its joint orbit.
Restriction and extension preserve constants, sums and products,
all evaluated pointwise. For an orbit #{C}, its indicator takes
value one on #{C} and zero elsewhere. Any assignment #{f} has
the finite expansion #{\sum_C f(C)\mathbf 1_C}. Evaluation on
each orbit recovers its coefficient, and evaluation gives the
stated multiplication rules.}
}

\p{Descent of an invariant map to orbits is formalized by
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.MulAction.orbitRelQuotientLift}.
The basis on the resulting finite function space is
\leanref{Pi.basisFun}.}
